eagues called =E2=80=9Cone of the most extensive technological blockades ev=
er attempted.=E2=80=9D It barred the sale of America=E2=80=99s most advance=
d A.I. chips directly to China.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But it didn=E2=80=99t take=
 long for various businesses to find ways around the restrictions. Megaspee=
d, which started out as a Chinese gaming company, spun off from its parent =
company to set up shop in Singapore in 2023.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In 2024, my c=
olleagues paid a visit to a market in the southern Chinese city of Shenzhen=
 and found vendors selling the very chips the U.S. was trying to keep out o=
f Chinese hands. One vendor shared evidence that he had recently shipped a =
batch of servers with more than 2,000 of Nvidia=E2=80=99s most advanced chi=
ps into China for more than $100 million.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Smuggling and un=
derground markets were one route through which Chinese companies gained acc=
ess to these chips; front companies were another. My colleagues have painst=
akingly documented instances of ties between newly founded companies and th=
e Chinese military, and links between Chinese parent companies and foreign =
subsidiaries that allowed them to avoid American blacklists.</span></p><p s=
tyle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><sp=
an>The Trump administration has </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/=
newsletter/pJhyLlNGbUKNSlC7BBX97Q~~/AAAAARA~/ZyieJfj1REmxrlovSYMv5rAxJnNejr=
